\title{Quantum collapse: the draw that makes counterfactuals real}
\taxon{reference}
\import{macros}
\author{monaduck1069}
\date{2026-07-17}
\tag{quantum}
\tag{voting}
\tag{bci.red}
\tag{bci.blue}

\p{The daily red/blue draw ([[bcf-devices]]) is currently a deterministic
date-seeded PRNG in the vote-agg Worker (FNV-1a #{\to} Mulberry32). This tree
argues that determinism voids the possible-world semantics ([[bci-0015]],
[[horse-0001]]) and identifies the physical fix: vacuum phase-diffusion QRNG
(Abellán et al., \emph{Opt. Express} 22(2):1645, 2014), 43 Gbps from a
gain-switched DFB laser + unbalanced Mach-Zehnder.}

\subtree{\title{Determinism has no counterfactuals}
\p{Under \code{getSeededRandom(dateStr)} the date fixes the pair. There is one
realized world per day and it pre-exists in the seed — so "all counterfactuals
for bci.blue" ([[bci-scan-connection]]) is a category error against a PRNG: the
superposition never collapses because it was a lookup, not a measurement.
Counterfactuals are real only if the draw is genuinely stochastic.
#{\langle\Delta\theta^2\rangle > (9.45\,\mathrm{rad})^2} is what "could have
gone otherwise" costs in physics.}
}

\subtree{\title{The identity/collapse split}
\taxon{remark}
\p{Not everything should become quantum. The forest already runs two clocks:}
\table{
\tr{\th{layer} \th{needs} \th{mechanism} \th{trit}}
\tr{\td{name #{\to} color (SplitMix64)} \td{persistence of self — your name must \emph{always} give your color} \td{deterministic hash ([[bci-0002]], passport.gay [[bcf-0024]])} \td{identity}}
\tr{\td{daily red/blue draw} \td{genuine indeterminacy — the measurement that collapses} \td{QRNG vacuum phase diffusion} \td{choice}}
}
\p{Determinism where you need to remain yourself; indeterminism where a real
choice is made. The \strong{place} crossover (trit 0) is exactly where the coin
is physically flipped — the QRNG sits at place, like the scanner
([[bci-scan-connection]]) sits at place. Both are trit-0 substrate devices.}
}

\subtree{\title{Reproducibility tension → committed beacon}
\taxon{remark}
\p{A QRNG breaks the Worker's one virtue: \emph{same date #{\to} same pair,
verifiable on any engine}. The resolution is the beacon pattern (NIST
randomness beacon / drand): at 00:00 UTC draw quantum bits, \strong{publish +
hash-commit} them, then derive the pair. Unpredictable-before, verifiable-after.
This is precisely what \code{final_state_hash} was reaching for and throwing
away ([[bcf-devices]] handoff): the commitment layer that makes the collapse
both genuine \emph{and} auditable. QRNG + committed beacon closes the
narrative-vs-runtime gap in one move.}
\p{Surety model comes straight from the paper: publish only the
min-entropy-certified quantum bits (their §6.2, 7.33/14 bits, Whirlpool
extractor RF #{\approx} 1.9). A beacon that emits only quantum-certified bits is
a beacon nobody — not even the operator — could have biased.}
}

\subtree{\title{As a device}
\taxon{remark}
\p{Buildable from telecom parts (1550 nm DFB LD w/ 10 Gbps mod + internal 30 dB
isolator, two 50/50 PM couplers, 3.55 cm PM-fiber delay for 5.825 GHz PRF, fast
photodetector, 14-bit digitizer). Sibling to the NV magnetometer ([[bcf-0031]],
tag \code{quantum}). Status \code{aspirational}: the fast digitizer/scope is the
cost driver, not the optics. GF(3): the extracted uniform stream maps to trits
#{\{-1,0,+1\}}, feeding the blue/place/red conservation law directly.}
}

\subtree{\title{The .05, tripled}
\taxon{remark}
\p{A counterfactual world \emph{exists} iff all three hold: (a) physically
resolvable — 0.05 mm head scan ([[bci-scan-connection]]); (b) genuinely
drawable — QRNG makes the selection stochastic; (c) statistically
distinguishable — separable at #{p<0.05} (cf. the paper's NIST battery at
#{\alpha_{SL}=0.01}). The scanner supplies (a), the QRNG supplies (b), and the
vote tally tested at significance supplies (c). Only worlds passing all three
are real counterfactuals; the rest are nominal.}
}
